{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Dinca1996-basic-stack",
  "Source": "A. Dinca, M. E. Trifan, V. Lupei, and M. Dinca, \"Dichroic mirror design by complete admittance matching,\" Opt. Eng. 35(5), 1284-1287 (1996). doi:10.1117/1.600667 - Sec. 3, p. 1286 ('The basic stack was chosen to have the structure (0.5L H 0.5L)^7'), spectrum in Fig. 1",
  "Notes": "Basic high-reflectance stack (0.5L H 0.5L)^7 alone, chosen for its low ripple on the short-wavelength side (Fig. 1)\nSpecification: High reflectance at 1064 nm with low ripple in the short-wavelength region (before matching).\nReported: Equivalent refractive index of the period at 532 nm Eb = 1.176 (Sec. 3). Fig. 1 (0.4-2.0 um): reflectance band near 1 from ~0.95 to ~1.2 um, small ripple below ~0.8 um, larger side lobes on the long-wavelength side. Recomputation check (this extraction): E of the (0.5L H 0.5L) period at 532 nm = 1.181.\nNotes: Fig. 1 does not state the media; assumed air incidence on the same BK7 substrate (n = 1.52) as the final design. QWOT at 1064 nm. (0.5L H 0.5L)^7 merged to 0.5L (H L)^6 H 0.5L, 15 layers. Normal incidence assumed.\nLayers from Sec. 3, p. 1286 ('The basic stack was chosen to have the structure (0.5L H 0.5L)^7'), spectrum in Fig. 1; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R 99.0 % at 1064 nm, 4.26 % at 532 nm; paper the basic stack, with the 532 nm ripple the matching layers remove (Fig. 1); Eb 1.176 at 532 nm.",
